WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.060
In this video, you give a new Linux virtual machine cloud-init user data that creates a user, installs packages and runs a command at first boot. Then you check the result inside the guest.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:18.620
On the Virtual Machines page, select Create to start a new virtual machine.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.360
Choose the demo namespace for this virtual machine,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.740
and give it the name demo-cloudinit, so you can find it later.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.810
Give the virtual machine one virtual CPU to start with,

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:33.790
and two gigabytes of memory, enough for this small test.

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.910
Open the Volumes tab to choose the boot image.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:46.530
Select the openSUSE Leap cloud image. It ships with cloud-init, which reads the user data at first boot.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:51.780
Then open Advanced Options, where the Cloud Configuration section holds the user data.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.530
The default user data only installs the guest agent. Replace it with your own.

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:06.070
The users key adds a user named tux with a password. The packages key installs three packages, and runcmd writes a login message and starts the guest agent.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:12.900
Select Create, and the cluster creates the virtual machine with this user data.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:21.010
The virtual machine is running. On its first boot, cloud-init now applies the user data inside the guest.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:24.960
When first boot is done, open the Console menu in its row

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.020
and select Open in WebVNC to see the guest screen.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:35.890
At the login prompt, type tux, the user that cloud-init created,

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.940
and the password from the user data.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:44.680
The login works, and the message from the runcmd command appears right after it.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:51.960
Now check the user and the packages. tux is in the wheel group, and all three packages are installed.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:58.470
cloud-init status confirms that first boot finished, with no errors.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:05.720
Back in the Virtual Machines list, demo-cloudinit keeps running, set up entirely from its user data.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:16.274
cloud-init created the user, installed the packages and ran the command on first boot, with no manual steps. Next, you prepare a Windows image with sysprep.
